use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Filtering:
    description:
        Filtering narrows a set of options to those matching a query and orders
        what remains. It sits between a text input and the surface rendering the
        results, and belongs to the component owning both rather than to either
        one of them.

    query:
        The text the user has entered. An empty query matches every option and
        preserves the source order.

    matching:
        An option matches when the normalised query occurs anywhere within its
        normalised label as a contiguous substring. Prefix matching and
        subsequence matching are deliberate alternatives to this rule, not
        variations to be selected per component.

    normalisation:
        Query and label are compared case-insensitively and with diacritics
        removed, so `cafe` matches `Café`. Whitespace surrounding the query is
        ignored; whitespace within it is significant.

    ordering:
        Matches are ordered by the position at which they matched, earliest
        first, with ties broken by the order of the source. Matching options are
        not reordered on any other basis.

    matchPositions:
        A match reports the range it occupied within the label, so that whatever
        renders the result can emphasise it. A matcher returning only a boolean
        cannot support this and does not satisfy the contract.

    emptyResult:
        A query matching nothing yields an empty result rather than falling back
        to the unfiltered set. What to render in that case is the owning
        component's decision.

    source:
        The option set is synchronous and known in full at the time of the
        query. Sourcing options remotely is a separate concern, carrying
        debouncing, cancellation, and loading and error states, and is not part
        of this behaviour.

    nonGoals:
        - No subsequence or fuzzy matching. It requires a ranking function to be
          usable, which is a larger decision than this behaviour should make on
          a component's behalf
        - No automatic highlighting of the first match. Whether Enter selects
          without an explicit move through the results belongs to the owning
          component
